<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      An object $C$ is $k$-truncated if and only if the morphism $C \rightarrow 1$ to the final object is $k$-truncated. Since $F$ preserves final object, it suffices to prove the assertion concerning morphisms. Since $F$ commutes with fiber products, Lemma <a href="/tag/5C585C" data-tag="5C585C">5.5.6.15</a> allows us to use induction on $k$, thereby reducing to the case where $k=-2$. But the $(-2)$-truncated morphisms are precisely the equivalences, and these are preserved by any functor. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>